=== Background and Rationale

Progressive post-exertional malaise (PEM) worsening may reflect cumulative biological damage, but the mechanism is unproven. TSPO (translocator protein) positron emission tomography (PET) can quantify a glial-activation signal in vivo, with three constraints. First, the ME/CFS studies are small and disagree: one positive cohort (n=9) and one null cohort (n=9) @Nakatomi2014neuroinflammation @Raijmakers2021neuroinflammation. Second, TSPO is expressed by microglia, astrocytes, endothelium, and vascular smooth muscle, so binding does not identify a cell type @Nutma2023TSPO. Third, second-generation tracers are sensitive to the rs6971 genotype. A post-COVID cohort reported a subgroup-level cerebral signal with no severity correlation; the group difference did not survive false-discovery-rate correction @Visser2025TSPOfatigue. This study would test whether a TSPO signal tracks PEM trajectory in ME/CFS. It would not establish TSPO-PET as a biomarker.


